Date: Thu, 16 Jul 1998 11:32:49 -0700
From: tvedt@noao.edu (Janet Tvedt)
Message-Id: <199807161832.LAA09040@kiwi.tuc.noao.edu>
To: gemini-software@gemini.edu
Subject: Motor record problems & fixes
Content-Type: X-sun-attachment
Sender: gemini-software-owner@noao.edu
Precedence: bulk
Reply-To: gemini-software@noao.edu
Status: RO
X-Status: 

----------
X-Sun-Data-Type: text
X-Sun-Data-Description: text
X-Sun-Data-Name: text
X-Sun-Charset: us-ascii
X-Sun-Content-Lines: 85

To motor record users:


I obtained a copy of the EPICS motor record from Hubert Yamada and Len Lawrence.
If you are using this record, there are some potential problems you should be 
aware of.  The first one is quite serious.  

1)  A monitor is posted on the motor status field (MSTA) every time the record
is processed even if the field has not changed.  The IOC developer's guide says
that it is *important* that record support modules *not* call db_post_event for
fields that do not change.  No reason is given, but I suppose each call broadcasts
a message over the network and unnecessary calls could flood the network.

The fix is to make the following modifications to recMotor.c
	A)  Eliminate the MARK(M_MSTA) from the process_motor_info function
	B)  Replace the call to update_values that is currently in the process 
	function with the code segment below:

    /* Update structure oms_trans which is pointed to by the DPVT (device private) field. 
       This is a call to the device support to obtain the motor status, velocity and position
       information.  Save previous motor status and post event if the status is changed by the
       device support.
    */
    oldStatus = pmr->msta;
    process_reason = (*pdset->update_values) (pmr);
    if(oldStatus != pmr->msta) MARK(M_MSTA);


2)  The first call to process() looks like a device-support callback and causes the
first move (or home) command to fail.

I fixed this by modifying the device support devMotorOms.c to change a line in the
function oms_init_callback to set the callback_changed variable to NO.  See below.


    /*  Setting this to YES meant that a write to a passive process field
        was ignored if it occurred just after startup.  Janet Tvedt
    */
    /*    ptrans->callback_changed = YES; */
    ptrans->callback_changed = NO;


3)  I suspect that there are other callback related problems which unnecessarily
complicate the record support code.  There appears to be a lot of duplicate
code in the process function that attempts to get around the problem.  The record
should be processed periodically due to callbacks from the driver.  The record
should also be processed whenever a PP field is written to.  It is possible to 
write to a PP field but still have the device support indicate that the record
was processed due to a callback.  Thus, the code to handle PP fields appears in
the if block that processes callback updates and the else block where it should
be.  I still do not have a handle on how to fix this properly.

4)  When a limit violation occurs, the auxillary output is not turned off.

5)  I found significant roundoff errors in the computation of the motor position
(actually a relative move) sent to the motor.  I do not recall how far off the
move distances were in my application, but it was enough that I fixed the problem
by changing the appropriate fields to double precision.

6)  If you use encoders, the position maintenance mode provided by the OMS controllers
is not supported.  You will need to make changes to the record support, device support
and a minor change to the driver to add this feature.

7)  The limit switch field (LLS) is reset even though the hardware switch is still
active.  See the first attachment below.  I commented out the section of code in
the driver that causes the problem.  The maintainers of the motor record have never
determined if this is a bug or a feature.  

8)  If you use encoders you may want to look at the other problem I mention in 
the first attachment where the readback value stored in the record did not match 
what was returned by the hardware.  This was never addressed by anyone.  Perhaps
they could not duplicate the problem. 

9)  If you use encoders, there is a section of code which may cause false limit
violations.  See the second attachment.  This problem was fixed in the EPICS 3.13
version of the record. 


Janet

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Janet Tvedt, Engineering Technical Services     Internet: tvedt@noao.edu
National Optical Astronomy Observatories        Phone:    (520) 318-8388
950 N. Cherry Ave., Tucson, AZ  85719           FAX:      (520) 318-8360

